<h2>How maintenance cases reach the Lok Adalat</h2>
<p>Two streams feed the forum. Pending cases — maintenance applications under Section 144 of the Bharatiya Nagarik Suraksha Sanhita, interim maintenance in divorce proceedings, monetary relief claims under the DV Act — are referred with the parties&rsquo; consent by the court seized of them, typically to a National Lok Adalat date. Pre-litigation disputes can also be placed before a Permanent Lok Adalat or taken up on an application to the legal services authority. In either stream, the essence is identical: the Lok Adalat has no power to adjudicate; it can only record a settlement the parties actually reach.</p>

<h2>The anatomy of the award</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>Deemed decree.</strong> Section 21 of the Legal Services Authorities Act, 1987 gives the award the status of a civil court decree, executable as such.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Finality.</strong> The award is final and binding; no appeal lies. The pending case stands disposed of in terms of the settlement.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>No court fee.</strong> Proceedings attract no fee, and in referred cases the court fee already paid on the petition is refundable.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Consent is the jurisdiction.</strong> Because the award records agreement, the absence of genuine consent is the only structural vice that can undermine it — and establishing that is deliberately difficult.</div>
</div>

<h2>Drafting the settlement: where cases are won years later</h2>
<p>Most post-settlement litigation traces back to loose drafting on the settlement day. A maintenance settlement should specify: the monthly amount and the date of each month by which it is payable; the mode (bank transfer to a named account); whether the amount covers the wife alone or children too, with separate figures for each; the treatment of arrears up to the settlement date; the relationship of the settled amount to any parallel orders under other statutes, so double recovery claims cannot arise; any agreed escalation (an annual percentage increase is common and enforceable as part of the decree); and the consequences of default. Where the settlement is a composite one — resolving DV, maintenance and divorce proceedings together — each case and its disposal terms should be listed by number.</p>
<div class="note"><p>A settlement that says only &ldquo;respondent shall pay Rs 15,000 per month&rdquo; leaves open every question that matters: from when, until when, for whom, and what happens on default. The Lok Adalat bench will record whatever the parties place before it; the discipline of completeness is counsel&rsquo;s.</p></div>

<h2>The room left for change</h2>
<p>Maintenance differs from a one-time money settlement because the obligation is inherently continuing and the law refuses to freeze it against reality. Section 146 of the BNSS permits alteration of a maintenance allowance on proof of a change in the circumstances of either party; Section 25(2) of the DV Act performs the same office for orders under that Act. A settlement recorded as a Lok Adalat award fixes the arrangement as the agreed order — but the alteration jurisdiction operates on orders, and a genuinely changed world can be brought back before the court. Expect scrutiny: a husband seeking reduction months after agreeing to a figure, or a wife seeking enhancement without any intervening event, will be asked what has changed since the award. The change must be real, material and post-settlement.</p>

<h2>Default and enforcement</h2>
<p>Where payments stop, the recipient is not remitted to a fresh suit. The award is executed as a decree, and in Section 144 BNSS matters the Magistrate&rsquo;s enforcement powers — including warrants for levying unpaid amounts — remain the fast lane. Arrears should be pursued promptly and each default documented; enforcement, like maintenance itself, rewards the organised litigant.</p>
<p>This article is for general information only and is not legal advice or a solicitation.</p>
